course: dictionary
lesson: 1242
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. The dictionary gives three meanings for bunch, and the sentence around it tells you which one to use. Look at the clues first.
explain: «A bunch of bananas» means a group of things held together, like fruit on a stalk. «A bunch of friends» is different: here bunch is informal and means a group of people, or a lot of something.
explain: To bunch up is a verb, an action word. It means to come close together in a group. In «the frightened sheep bunch up and move away», the sheep crowd together before they run off. Check your meaning by putting it into the sentence and seeing if it still makes sense.
fact: A bunch is a group of things held together, such as a bunch of grapes, keys or flowers.
fact: In everyday talk, «a bunch of» can mean a group of people, or a lot of something.
fact: To bunch up means to come close together in a group.
fact: «The best of the bunch» means the best one out of a group.
fact: Bunch can be a noun (a bunch, bunches) or a verb (to bunch, bunched, bunching).
exercise: «The hands you draw look like bunches of bananas.» In this sentence, what is a bunch? | A sharp tool for cutting | A very large, heavy box | A group of people, like friends | * A group of things held together, like fruit
exercise: «The frightened sheep bunch up and move away from the dog.» What does bunch up mean here? | * To come close together in a group | To fall asleep in the grass | To shout loudly at each other | To run very fast on your own
exercise: In «the lines bunch up at the poles», the lines are close together. Which phrase from the dictionary means the best one out of a group? | Bunch up | A bunch of friends | * The best of the bunch | Bunches of grapes
test: «He has a bunch of friends coming to his party.» Here, bunch means what? | A type of fruit | * A group of people, informal | A bunch of keys | To come close together
test: «Iron filings bunch up at the poles, where the pull is strongest.» What does this tell you? | Iron filings fly away from the magnet | * The filings crowd together where the pull is strongest | The filings sparkle in the light | The filings turn into dust
exam: Maya says bunch can only mean fruit. Is she right? | Yes, bunch only ever means a group of fruit | Yes, but only when it is a verb | No, bunch can also mean a group of people, or to come close together in a group | * No, bunch can also mean a group of people, or to come close together in a group
